Yeah, I noticed that the set of tiles had duplicates for each dungeon
branch, but that wasn't pertinent to the OP's described problem, and I
was already long past the point where the clarity of my exposition
needed more complexity to obscure matters! <grin>

That's almost certainly why the other.txt tiles come _last_, and thus
at the bottom of the combined eventual GIF or bitmap file; if one wants
to add another dungeon branch and corresponding tiles for it, at least
the other two sets of tiles go unrelocated by the action.

It's actually kind of spiffy that the wall tiles can be different for
each branch of the dungeon, but it's a bit limiting that the drawbridge
up and down indices share the wall vertical and horizontal indices, so
that the same tile design must suffice for both; someone looking for a
medium difficulty project might want to (with appropriate ifdefs so as
not to break all existing third party tile sets) go in and disambiguate
the drawbridge and wall icons for future tile designers.  And of course
for text, ambiguate them right back.

Open Source software: what can you say about it?  Wonderful and awful
